        <h1>THREE DATABASES, ONE STUDY</h1>
        <p class="papel">How I turned three databases from a clinical study, each with a different questionnaire, into a single dataset, and set up a standard so future data collection would arrive organized.</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="ficha">
        <dt>Type</dt><dd>Product case study</dd>
        <dt>Company</dt><dd>Cignamed, internship</dd>
        <dt>Period</dt><dd>May – Oct 2026</dd>
        <dt>Tools</dt><dd>Python (pandas), Excel, REDCap</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <p class="aviso">Project carried out during my internship at Cignamed, in a clinical research study. No participant data appears here: the case covers only the problem and the process.</p>

    <section class="secao">
      <h2>OUTCOME</h2>
      <ul>
        <li class="linha"><strong>2 MONTHS TO 2 MIN</strong><p>Merging the databases, which took about two months of work, now takes around two minutes.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>950K ANSWERS</strong><p>About 1,900 respondents and 500 questions brought together in a single dataset, without losing any question that was collected.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>200+ DUPLICATES</strong><p>Duplicates and hundreds of inconsistencies (out-of-range dates and ages, badly formatted values) found and fixed, with every correction recorded in a log.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>NEW STANDARD</strong><p>Later interviews started being collected in REDCap, already in the format of the merged dataset, so the problem would not come back.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="secao">
      <h2>THE PROBLEM</h2>
      <ul>
        <li class="linha"><strong>CONTEXT</strong><p>I was brought into the internship precisely to solve this problem. The lead researcher had three databases from the same study, each with a different questionnaire, and needed everything combined: the questions and the respondents.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>WHY CODE ALONE WASN’T ENOUGH</strong><p>At first it looked like a job for a script, but the questions were written differently in each database, with typos and variations. Code had no way of knowing that two columns were the same question.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>WHO FELT IT</strong><p>The research team, who needed the complete dataset to analyze the study and depended on slow manual work to get there.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="secao">
      <h2>DISCOVERY AND DECISIONS</h2>
      <ul>
        <li class="linha"><strong>STUDY BEFORE CODING</strong><p>Before writing any code, I studied the research material to understand each question and find out which ones matched across the databases, even when they didn’t follow the pattern.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>FIX IT AT THE SOURCE</strong><p>I standardized the questions in all three databases. Only once they were written the same way did the automatic merge start working.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>NOTHING GETS LOST</strong><p>I merged the databases with pandas. Questions that existed in only one database were not dropped: they went to the end of the spreadsheet, so the final dataset keeps everything that was collected.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>KEEP IT FROM COMING BACK</strong><p>So that future interviews would follow the same standard, I built the forms in REDCap, a tool widely used by researchers, with field types and validation rules that block errors at collection time.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="secao">
      <h2>LESSONS LEARNED</h2>
      <ul>
        <li class="linha"><strong>CONTENT COMES FIRST</strong><p>The hardest part wasn’t technical: question quality varied a lot from one database to another. Understanding the content is what unlocked the solution.</p></li>
        <li class="linha"><strong>WHAT I’D DO DIFFERENTLY</strong><p>I would test other data collection tools before adopting REDCap, comparing each one against what the team needed day to day.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

